(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-3-endocrine-quiz-version-4"]={"title":"Endocrine System — Anatomy Exam 3 (Set 4)","category":"Anatomy","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which endocrine gland undergoes atrophy during adulthood?","choices":["Adrenal","Thyroid","Pancreas","Thymus"],"answer":3,"exp":"The thymus (source of thymosins) undergoes atrophy during adulthood, unlike the other endocrine organs. — Slide 5 (Figure 19.1 — Thymus)"},{"q":"The posterior lobe of the pituitary gland is also known as the:","choices":["Pars distalis","Adenohypophysis","Neurohypophysis","Pars tuberalis"],"answer":2,"exp":"The neurohypophysis is the posterior lobe of the pituitary, releasing oxytocin and ADH that are produced in the hypothalamus. — Slide 10 (The Pituitary Gland)"},{"q":"The adenohypophysis releases how many peptide hormones?","choices":["Five","Two","Nine","Twelve"],"answer":2,"exp":"The anterior lobe (adenohypophysis) releases nine peptide hormones, reflecting its five cell types (somatotropes, lactotropes, thyrotropes, corticotropes, and gonadotropes). — Slide 10 (The Pituitary Gland)"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT one of the main (primary) endocrine organs?","choices":["Pituitary gland","Adipose tissue","Thyroid gland","Adrenal glands"],"answer":1,"exp":"Adipose tissue is a secondary endocrine tissue (leptin, resistin); the pituitary, thyroid, and adrenal glands are primary endocrine organs. — Slides 3–4 (Overview)"},{"q":"The suprarenal cortex is subdivided into how many distinct zones?","choices":["Five","Two","Four","Three"],"answer":3,"exp":"The adrenal cortex has three zones: glomerulosa, fasciculata, and reticularis. — Slide 82 (The Suprarenal Glands)"},{"q":"Which endocrine structure is located on the posterior surface of the thyroid gland?","choices":["Pineal gland (epiphysis)","Parathyroid glands","Anterior pituitary gland","Cervical thymus gland"],"answer":1,"exp":"The parathyroid glands lie on the posterior surface of the thyroid gland; sometimes parathyroid tissue is embedded within the thyroid. — Slide 43 (Parathyroid Glands)"},{"q":"The reproductive glands (gonads) function as endocrine organs by secreting:","choices":["Insulin and glucagon","Sex steroids and inhibin","Calcitonin and PTH","Epinephrine and cortisol"],"answer":1,"exp":"The gonads secrete sex steroids (androgens or estrogens/progestins) and inhibin, functioning as endocrine organs. — Slide 5 (Figure 19.1 — Gonads)"},{"q":"Which endocrine tissue of the heart secretes natriuretic peptides (ANP, BNP)?","choices":["Cardiac tissue (a secondary endocrine tissue)","The pericardium (fibrous outer layer)","The anterior pituitary (pars distalis)","The adrenal cortex (zona glomerulosa)"],"answer":0,"exp":"The heart is a secondary endocrine tissue that secretes atrial and brain natriuretic peptides (ANP and BNP). — Slides 4–5 (Overview / Figure 19.1)"},{"q":"Within the pars distalis, chromophobes are distinguished from chromophils in that chromophobes:","choices":["Have no granules in their cytoplasm","Have deeply reddish, acidophilic cytoplasm","Secrete parathyroid hormone actively","Form the colloid-filled thyroid follicle"],"answer":0,"exp":"Chromophobes (~50% of pars distalis cells) lack cytoplasmic granules; chromophils (acidophils and basophils) contain granules. — Slide 21 (Pars distalis)"},{"q":"The central mass of material stored within a thyroid follicle is called the:","choices":["Chromaffin granule","Herring body","Zymogen granule","Colloid"],"answer":3,"exp":"Each thyroid follicle surrounds a central mass of colloid, which stores thyroid hormone precursor. — Slides 30, 40 (Thyroid Follicles showing Colloid)"},{"q":"The zona fasciculata of the adrenal cortex is composed of lipid-laden cells arranged in radial columns and secretes:","choices":["Glucocorticoids (cortisol)","Mineralocorticoids (aldosterone)","Androgens","Catecholamines"],"answer":0,"exp":"The zona fasciculata secretes glucocorticoids (cortisol); its cells are lipid-droplet-laden and arranged in radial columns. — Slide 87 (Histology of Adrenal Glands)"},{"q":"The zona reticularis of the adrenal cortex secretes which class of hormones?","choices":["Mineralocorticoids","Androgens","Glucocorticoids","Catecholamines"],"answer":1,"exp":"The zona reticularis, arranged in a loose meshwork, secretes androgens. — Slide 87 (Histology of Adrenal Glands)"},{"q":"In the pars distalis, basophils (bluish cytoplasm) secrete all of the following EXCEPT:","choices":["Thyrotropin (TSH)","Growth hormone","Corticotropin (ACTH)","Follitropin (FSH)"],"answer":1,"exp":"Basophils secrete TSH, ACTH, FSH, and LH; growth hormone (and prolactin) are secreted by acidophils. — Slide 21 (Pars distalis)"},{"q":"The chromaffin cells of the adrenal medulla are best described as:","choices":["Modified postganglionic sympathetic neurons","Modified thyroid follicular epithelial cells","Modified renal glomerular podocytes","Modified parathyroid chief cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Chromaffin cells are modified postganglionic sympathetic neurons that lack dendrites and axons and secrete catecholamines. — Slide 89 (Adrenal Medulla)"},{"q":"On fixation, the homogeneous intercellular substance of the pars nervosa precipitates to form:","choices":["Colloid","Herring bodies","Zymogen granules","Reticular fibers"],"answer":1,"exp":"In the pars nervosa, the homogeneous intercellular substance precipitates on fixation to form Herring bodies. — Slide 22 (Pars nervosa)"},{"q":"The medial surface of the thyroid gland is related to the larynx, pharynx, and which nerve?","choices":["External laryngeal nerve","Facial (cranial nerve VII)","Phrenic nerve of the neck","Spinal accessory nerve"],"answer":0,"exp":"The medial surface relates to the larynx, pharynx, and external laryngeal nerve, plus the trachea, esophagus, and recurrent laryngeal nerve. — Slide 27 (Thyroid medial surface)"},{"q":"The neck of the pancreas lies anterior to which vessel, with the pylorus just above it?","choices":["The abdominal aorta directly","The inferior vena cava wall","The superior mesenteric vein (SMV)","The splenic artery and vein"],"answer":2,"exp":"The pancreatic neck lies anterior to the SMV, with the pylorus just above; the SMV outlines the medial head-to-neck region. — Slides 57, 76 (Pancreas relationships)"},{"q":"The body of the pancreas is related posteriorly to the left kidney, left adrenal, left renal vein, and which vessel?","choices":["Common bile duct","Portal vein","Superior mesenteric artery","Splenic vein"],"answer":3,"exp":"The pancreatic body relates posteriorly to the left crus, left adrenal, left renal vein, and splenic vein; the celiac trunk lies superior. — Slide 59 (Pancreas body relations)"},{"q":"The pituitary gland is related on either side to which venous structure?","choices":["The internal jugular vein","The cavernous sinus","The sigmoid sinus","The great cerebral vein"],"answer":1,"exp":"The pituitary is related on either side to the cavernous sinus, and behind to the dorsum sellae. — Slide 13 (Pituitary Gland)"},{"q":"The diaphragma sellae, a fold of dura over the pituitary, is pierced centrally by the infundibulum and separates the gland from the:","choices":["Sphenoid sinus","Cavernous sinus","Optic chiasma","Dorsum sellae"],"answer":2,"exp":"The diaphragma sellae is pierced by the infundibulum and separates the pituitary from the optic chiasma above. — Slide 13 (Pituitary Gland)"},{"q":"The head of the pancreas is clinically important in part because carcinoma is usually located there and because:","choices":["It filters blood of toxins directly","It lies fully intraperitoneal and mobile","It secretes calcitonin into the bloodstream","Numerous ducts and vessels traverse it"],"answer":3,"exp":"The pancreatic head is clinically important because numerous ducts and vessels traverse it and carcinoma is usually located there. — Slide 65 (Head of Pancreas)"},{"q":"The thyroid gland lies in the lower neck spanning approximately which vertebral levels?","choices":["T4 to T6","C1 to C3","C5 to T1","C7 to T4"],"answer":2,"exp":"The thyroid lies at the level of the 5th cervical to the 1st thoracic vertebrae. — Slide 25 (The Thyroid Gland)"},{"q":"Parathyroid hormone and calcitonin have what relationship in calcium regulation?","choices":["They are unrelated — each hormone regulates a different electrolyte entirely","They are synergistic — both PTH and calcitonin raise blood calcium levels","They are synergistic — both PTH and calcitonin lower blood calcium levels","They are antagonists — PTH raises and calcitonin lowers blood calcium"],"answer":3,"exp":"PTH raises blood calcium and calcitonin lowers it, making them functional antagonists in calcium homeostasis. — Slides 31, 44 (calcium regulation)"},{"q":"The kidney contributes to calcium metabolism by producing which hormone?","choices":["Calcitriol","Calcitonin","Parathyroid hormone","Aldosterone"],"answer":0,"exp":"The kidney produces calcitriol (active vitamin D), which raises calcium by increasing intestinal absorption. — Slide 5 (Figure 19.1 — Kidney)"},{"q":"Which cells would be MOST directly implicated if blood calcium became abnormally low and needed to be raised?","choices":["Thyroid parafollicular (C) cells","Parathyroid chief cells (secreting PTH)","Adrenal medullary chromaffin cells","Pancreatic beta cells (secreting insulin)"],"answer":1,"exp":"Low blood calcium stimulates parathyroid chief cells to secrete PTH, which raises blood calcium. — Slides 44, 46 (Parathyroid Glands)"},{"q":"Parafollicular C cells are separated from the follicular lumen by which structure?","choices":["The follicular epithelium","The colloid","The capsule","The basement membrane of a capillary"],"answer":0,"exp":"C cells lie near the follicular basement lamina but are separated from the follicle lumen by the follicular epithelium. — Slide 31 (Parafollicular / C cells)"},{"q":"Adipose tissue is frequently found within which calcium-regulating gland, especially in older individuals?","choices":["Adrenal cortex tissue","Thyroid gland tissue","Parathyroid gland","Pineal gland tissue"],"answer":2,"exp":"Adipose tissue is frequently found within the parathyroid gland in older individuals. — Slide 46 (Histology of parathyroid glands)"},{"q":"Compared with the acidophils, the basophils of the pars distalis generally:","choices":["Form the colloid","Are smaller and lack granules","Secrete growth hormone","Are larger and stain deeper"],"answer":3,"exp":"Basophils are generally larger and stain deeper than acidophils, and secrete TSH, ACTH, FSH, and LH. — Slide 21 (Pars distalis)"},{"q":"The thyroid isthmus lies opposite which tracheal rings?","choices":["The 2nd and 3rd tracheal rings","The 4th and 5th tracheal rings","The 1st tracheal ring only","The 6th and 7th tracheal rings"],"answer":0,"exp":"The thyroid isthmus lies opposite the 2nd and 3rd tracheal rings, covered by the sternohyoid and sternothyroid muscles. — Slide 29 (Thyroid isthmus)"},{"q":"The pointed tail of the pancreas extends toward which organ?","choices":["The stomach","The liver","The spleen","The right kidney"],"answer":2,"exp":"The pancreatic tail extends toward the hilum of the spleen; the rounded head connects to the duodenum. — Slide 50 (The Pancreas)"}]};
